;; The blocking lint: which bjoroutines end up holding a thread.
;;
;; A fiber that waits is supposed to give its thread back to the scheduler.
;; A fiber that calls something which *parks* does not — it sits on a pool
;; thread for the duration, and every other fiber has one fewer to run on. That
;; is legal, occasionally deliberate, and invisible: nothing goes wrong until
;; the pool is empty, at which point the program stops for reasons nothing in it
;; explains.
;;
;; So it is a warning, and the warning names the path. Every case below compiles
;; — that is half the assertion, since a lint that became an error would pass a
;; test looking only for the text.

(import (std prelude))

;; --- NOT reported: reached through an ordinary function ----------------------
;;
;; This used to be the headline case, and it is now the headline case for the
;; opposite reason. Neither `drain` nor `worker` mentions a thread, and neither
;; has to: `drain` reaches a `defbjouble`, so it is given a suspending copy, and
;; `worker` calls that copy. Nothing parks, so there is nothing to say.
;;
;; What the lint reported here was never a mistake in the program — it was the
;; compiler declining to generate a body. Generating it is the fix, and the
;; silence is the fix landing.

(: drain (-> TextInputPort int))
(defun (drain p)
  (let loop ((n 0))
    (if (port-eof? p)
        n
        (let ()
          (ignore (read-line p))
          (loop (+ n 1))))))

(: worker (-bjo-> TextInputPort int))
(defbjo (worker p)
  (drain p))

;; --- Reported: where the copy cannot be reached ------------------------------
;;
;; `drain` has a suspending copy, and this cannot use it. A `(seq ...)` body is
;; a C# iterator, so a call inside one may not await, and selection leaves it on
;; the ordinary copy rather than rewriting it somewhere the await would be
;; illegal. The copy exists; there is nowhere here to call it from.
;;
;; So the warning survives exactly where the generated body does not reach, and
;; the path it names is the one still made of ordinary calls.

;; EXPECT-WARNING: 'lazily' is a bjoroutine
;; EXPECT-WARNING: lazily -> drain -> port-eof?
;; EXPECT-WARNING: parks the thread it runs on

(: lazily (-bjo-> TextInputPort (Seq int)))
(defbjo (lazily p)
  (seq (yield (drain p))))

;; --- Reported, and not as a bjoroutine ---------------------------------------
;;
;; The same shape as `lazily`, written `defun`. It still gets a suspending copy
;; — its call graph reaches a `defbjouble` — and the copy still parks, for the
;; same reason: the call it would have to await sits inside a `(seq ...)`.
;;
;; So the verdict is the same and the sentence must not be. Calling this a
;; bjoroutine would be false about a definition the reader wrote `defun`, and
;; `humanize` strips the `__bjo` before this prints, so there would be nothing
;; on the page to reveal that the message was about a copy. The advice differs
;; too, and has to: `sync` is a yield point, so `(sync (blocking ...))` cannot
;; be written in a `defun` body at all.

;; EXPECT-WARNING: 'lazy-drain' is not a bjoroutine, but the suspending copy of it
;; EXPECT-WARNING: lazy-drain -> drain -> port-eof?
;; EXPECT-WARNING: write the suspending version by hand with (defbjo ...)

(: lazy-drain (-> TextInputPort (Seq int)))
(defun (lazy-drain p)
  (seq (yield (drain p))))

;; --- Reported: the leaf called directly --------------------------------------
;;
;; `File.WriteAllText` waits on the thread it is called on, is imported saying
;; so, and has nothing between it and the bjoroutine — so the path the warning
;; names is two names long.
;;
;; It stays reported because it stays true. Rewriting it over a port the way
;; `file-read-text` was rewritten needs an opener that appends, so that
;; `file-append-text` could follow it; until both can move, this is a wait the
;; lint is right to keep printing.

;; EXPECT-WARNING: 'writer' is a bjoroutine
;; EXPECT-WARNING: writer -> file-write-text

(: writer (-bjo-> string int))
(defbjo (writer path)
  (file-write-text path "hello")
  0)

;; --- Not reported: reading a whole file --------------------------------------
;;
;; `writer`'s shape, and silent, which is the whole of what the read side
;; gained. `file-read-text` opens a port and drains it, `read-all` is one of the
;; leaf pairs, so a suspending copy of `file-read-text` exists and this call
;; reaches it: the fiber awaits the file instead of sitting on a pool thread
;; for the length of it.
;;
;; The *open* still parks, once, and nothing here says so — `StreamReader.`'s
;; constructor is not marked `#:blocking`, because a wait with no async form to
;; move to is not a finding, and marking it would print this path forever with
;; nothing to be done about it.

(: slurp (-bjo-> string int))
(defbjo (slurp path)
  (string-length (file-read-text path)))

;; --- Not reported: the fix ---------------------------------------------------
;;
;; The same work, moved onto a pool thread the runtime can grow to replace. The
;; fiber suspends on the *result* rather than on the read, so its own thread
;; goes back to the scheduler.
;;
;; This is the case the lint has to stay quiet about. It is the advice the
;; warning above gives, and a lint that fires on its own recommendation is one
;; nobody leaves switched on.
(: polite (-bjo-> TextInputPort int))
(defbjo (polite p)
  (match (sync (blocking (fun () (drain p))))
    ((Ok n) n)
    ((Err e) 0)))

;; --- Not reported: an ordinary function --------------------------------------
;;
;; `counter` parks the thread it is called on, and that thread is whatever
;; called it. There is no fiber to starve and nothing to report — this is what
;; a `defun` doing IO is supposed to look like.
(: counter (-> TextInputPort int))
(defun (counter p)
  (drain p))

(defun (main args)
  ;; Called so that nothing above is dead code, and to pin that a program the
  ;; lint has complained about still runs.
  (ignore (counter (open-input-string "one\ntwo\n")))
  0)
